'''Tainted Keeper''' is a [[character]] added in [[The Binding of Isaac: Repentance]]. He is the alternate version of {{C|Keeper}}. Tainted Keeper is unlocked by reaching {{s|Home}} and using the {{I|Red Key}}, {{P|Cracked Key}}, or {{P|1=Soul of Cain}} to access a special room in one of the walls while playing as Keeper.

Tainted Keeper starts with (and has a maximum of) 2 [[Health#Coin Hearts|coin hearts]] and 1 {{e|Bomb}}. Tainted Keeper shares the same mechanics with the regular Keeper, including recovering one coin heart upon picking up a coin, as well as converting any [[Hearts|heart pickups]] to [[blue flies]], {{dlc|r+}} except for {{e|Gold Heart}}s, which immediately turn into coins when picked up. Picking up a coin to restore a heart does not add a coin to his coin counter. Tainted Keeper shoots four tears at a time, similar to the item {{I|Mutant Spider}}. Whenever an enemy is defeated, a random coin is dropped that will vanish quickly.{{explain}} Most [[items]] encountered when playing as Tainted Keeper must first be paid for with coins, including Deals with the Devil, {{r|Angel Room}}, {{R|Boss Room}} items, and {{R|Treasure Room}} items.

== Strategy ==
* Tainted Keeper plays like a glass cannon: his ability to stockpile coins lets him purchase many more items per floor than most other characters, allowing him to potentially steamroll early floors. However, his two coin hearts (and very limited methods to increase max health) means he is unable to make many mistakes in comparison, especially for drawn-out boss fights without monster spawns.
* In the standard game mode, {{r|Shop}}s are different and much better for Tainted Keeper. They do not require a {{e|Key}} and generally have increased stock available. At max level, they have eight items/pickups for sale, of which there will always be at least one {{ip|Shop|text=Shop item}}, one {{ip|Boss|text=Boss Room item}}, and two {{ip|Treasure Room|text=Treasure Room items}}. The other four terminals in the shop can be keys, bombs, cards, pills, trinkets (which will be sold for 5 cents), or more Shop/Treasure Room items.
** This shop can rarely be replaced by a better (or worse) variant: The better variant has 2-6 more shopkeepers, [[Rocks#Urns|Urns]] scattered about, two {{E|Lucky Penny|text=Lucky Pennies}}, and one more random item/pickup for sale. The worse variant has only one random sale terminal, one {{e|Shopkeeper}}, some {{e|Poop}}, and a {{E|Fly}}.
** These improved shops do not appear in {{mode|Greed}} [[Greed Mode]].
** Due to shops including {{ip|Treasure Room|text=Treasure Room items}}, it is usually best to skip {{R|Treasure Room}}s for a {{R|Planetarium}}.
* Some items do not need to be paid for. These include:
** [[All Mini-Bosses (Bosses)|Mini-Boss]] drops.
** {{I|The Small Rock}} if dropped from a tinted rock.
** Golden and Red Chest items.
** Items spawned by beggars and machines.
** Items spawned by other items, such as through {{I|Eden's Soul}} or {{P|XVII - The Stars?}} card.
** Plot-related items, including {{I|The Polaroid}}, {{I|The Negative}}, {{I|Key Piece 1}}, {{I|Key Piece 2}}, {{I|Knife Piece 1}}, {{I|Knife Piece 2}}, and {{I|Dad's Note}}.
*** This also applies to drops generated from Angel Statues while holding {{t|Filigree Feather}}.
** Boss drops in {{s|The Void}}.
* Enemies spawned after entering the room (i.e. spawned by another enemy or spawned on later waves in [[Challenge Room]]s) don't have a guaranteed coin drop.
** Spawned enemies will stop dropping coins after fights go on long enough such as with {{R|Boss Rush}}.
* Champion enemies and [[Mini-boss|Mini-Bosses]] drop 2 coins on death.
* Bosses drop several coins.
** If the boss comprises multiple enemies (e.g. {{E|Gurglings}}), each of them drops fewer coins.
** Most degraded bosses drop 3 coins, exceptions such as {{E|The Cage}} and {{E|Monstro II}} have regular boss coin drops.
** Coins dropped by bosses stay on the floor longer before disappearing.
* {{R|Boss Rush}} items must still be paid for, and doing so will start the event.
* A high speed stat can help pick up coins before they disappear.
* A high fire rate will increase reliability in killing groups of enemies, allowing for safer coin collection. However, as changes to the Tears stat are much less effective on fire rate at or below -0.77 Tears (see [[Tears]] for the exact calculations), Tainted Keeper will gain very little fire rate from his first 1.43 added Tears. After crossing this threshold, as Tainted Keeper fires 4 tears at a time with no multiplicative fire rate penalty, he can gain far more DPS from Tears than other characters.
** For reference, a Tears Up pill gives 0.35 tears. Assuming no other tears modifiers are taken, Tainted Keeper will gain 0.07, 0.09, 0.10, and 0.12 fire rate from his first four Tears Up pills, and 0.52 from his fifth; more than his first four ''combined''.
** Items that directly add fire rate are very desirable for Tainted Keeper while his Tears stat is low. 
* Because Tainted Keeper has to pay for all items, taking devil deals is often more powerful than taking Angel items. This is because taking one devil deal item will not despawn the other items, but only one item can be taken from an Angel room.
